@node Main "AmiSync 0.9.4"

@{b}AmiSync@{ub} - a Syncthing-compatible file synchronization daemon for
AmigaOS 3.x (68020 and up)

  @{" Introduction    " link Intro}   what AmiSync and Syncthing are
  @{" Requirements    " link Require} what you need to run it
  @{" Installing      " link Install} unpacking and the Installer
  @{" Setting up      " link Setup}   pairing with your other devices
  @{" Running         " link Running} starting and stopping the daemon
  @{" Workbench       " link Workbench} the AppIcon and Tools menu
  @{" ARexx           " link ARexx}   scripting the daemon
  @{" Configuration   " link Config}  every S:amisync.conf option
  @{" Versioning      " link Version} the .stversions safety net
  @{" Troubleshooting " link Trouble} when something is not right
  @{" Credits         " link Credits} authors and licenses

AmiSync is an independent project, not affiliated with or endorsed by
Syncthing.

@endnode

@node Intro "Introduction"
@toc Main
@{b}What is Syncthing?@{ub}

Syncthing is a popular open-source program that keeps folders in sync
between two or more computers: save, change or delete a file on one
machine and the same thing happens on the others, over an encrypted
connection, with no cloud account in the middle. It runs on Windows,
macOS, Linux and phones.

@{b}What is AmiSync?@{ub}

AmiSync brings this to the classic Amiga. It speaks Syncthing's native
protocol (the Block Exchange Protocol over TLS 1.3, via AmiSSL), so the
Amiga appears as just another device in your Syncthing setup, and a
folder on the Amiga stays mirrored with a folder on any of your other
machines.

@{b}What works@{ub}

Files sync both ways with current Syncthing (v2.x): connections dialled
out or accepted inbound, deletions, directories, .stignore patterns,
per-block SHA-256 verification, conflict copies, rename/copy detection,
local discovery, and a persistent index that survives restarts.

@{b}Limits@{ub}

A folder tree nests at most 12 levels deep; entries deeper than that are
skipped. There is no fixed cap on the number of entries - the per-folder
index grows as needed, so folder size is bounded by RAM (every synced
file keeps an index record and its block hashes in memory).

@{b}What is not supported@{ub}

Global discovery and relays (peers must be directly reachable - the same
LAN or a dialable address), symlinks, and receive-encrypted/untrusted
peers.

@endnode

@node Require "Requirements"
@toc Main
To run AmiSync you need:

  o  AmigaOS 3.x on a 68020 or better. No FPU is needed (the crypto is
     all integer maths); on slower CPUs the same maths makes TLS
     handshakes take a while, so expect patience below a 68040.

  o  @{b}AmiSSL@{ub} 5.x installed (it provides TLS). Get it from
     https://github.com/jens-maus/amissl

  o  A running @{b}TCP/IP stack@{ub} providing bsdsocket.library -
     Roadshow, AmiTCP or Miami.

A few MB of free RAM is plenty for a normal folder.

@endnode

@node Install "Installing"
@toc Main
Download the latest amisync-<version>.lha, extract it on the Amiga, then
either double-click @{b}Install@{ub} or run it from a Shell in the
extracted drawer:

   Installer Install

The installer picks the build for your CPU (68020/68030, 68040 or
68060), copies @{b}amisync@{ub} and @{b}amisync-genid@{ub} into your
command path (default C:), installs the Workbench icon, seeds
@{b}S:amisync.conf@{ub} (leaving any existing one untouched), creates
this device's identity if AmiSSL is installed and none exists yet, and
can add AmiSync to S:User-Startup so it starts at every boot.

Once installed, continue with @{" Setting up " link Setup}.

@endnode

@node Setup "Setting up a sync"
@toc Main
@{b}1. Show the Amiga's device ID@{ub}:

   amisync-genid SHOWID

On your other machine, add the Amiga as a device in Syncthing - on the
same LAN it appears in the "Add Remote Device" suggestions, otherwise
type the ID - and share a folder with it.

@{b}2. Tell AmiSync which folder to sync@{ub}, in S:amisync.conf:

   ; folder id (must match the id in Syncthing), local path, mode:
   folder = amisync Work:Sync sendreceive

@{b}3. Start AmiSync@{ub} (see @{" Running " link Running}) - or reboot,
if the installer added it to User-Startup.

@{b}4. Add the other device as a peer.@{ub} On the same LAN this is a
click: it is discovered within moments and appears in the status
window's Remote Devices list with a "Discovered" badge - select it and
press @{b}Add...@{ub} (or use the ARexx @{"ADDPEER" link ARexx}
command). AmiSync only ever connects to peers you add - nothing is
accepted automatically.

No shared LAN, or prefer it explicit? Put the peer in S:amisync.conf
yourself:

   peer = P56IOI7-...-XZWICQ2 192.168.1.50:22000

Either way the device ID pins the TLS connection - a peer presenting any
other certificate is rejected. See @{" Configuration " link Config} for
every option.

@{b}Every folder is offered to every peer.@{ub} Unlike Syncthing, AmiSync
has no per-device folder sharing: a device you add can request any file in
any configured folder. Add only devices you would give all of those
folders to, and keep anything you would not on a separate machine.

@endnode

@node Running "Running"
@toc Main
Start AmiSync from a Shell:

   amisync            starts and detaches into the background
   amisync NODETACH   stays in the foreground (handy for debugging)

Stop it with Ctrl-C (foreground), from the Workbench Tools menu, or from
a Shell:

   rx "address AMISYNC QUIT"

If the installer added it to S:User-Startup, it starts automatically at
every boot.

@endnode

@node Workbench "Workbench"
@toc Main
While running, AmiSync shows an @{b}AppIcon@{ub} on the Workbench
backdrop whose label is the live sync status ("Up to Date", "Syncing (3
files)", "Offline"). Double-click it for a status window with the full
report, updated live every couple of seconds; double-clicking again brings
the open window to the front.

The status window shows a @{b}This Device@{ub} panel, then
@{b}Folders@{ub} and @{b}Remote Devices@{ub} as lists sorted by name,
each with a live @{b}State@{ub} column ("Up to Date", "Syncing (3
files)", "Scanning"). @{b}Click a row@{ub} to select it and unfold its
details beneath; click it again to collapse. The buttons under each
list act on the selected row and ghost when they do not apply.

Under Folders: @{b}Add...@{ub} (choose or create a drawer in the
requester, then name the folder in the small dialog - the name is its
label, the drawer's name its id), @{b}Open@{ub} (opens the drawer on
Workbench), @{b}Rescan@{ub} (just that folder), @{b}Accept...@{ub}
(configures a folder a peer offers - such rows carry an "Offered"
badge; pick the local drawer and syncing starts) and @{b}Remove...@{ub}
(stops syncing it - no files are deleted, here or on peers). Under
Remote Devices: @{b}Add...@{ub} (a device seen on the LAN - rows
badged "Discovered"), @{b}Pause@{ub}/@{b}Resume@{ub} (that device
only) and @{b}Remove...@{ub}. Anything destructive asks first.

Along the bottom: @{b}Rescan All@{ub}, @{b}Pause All@{ub}/@{b}Resume
All@{ub}, @{b}Open Log@{ub} (views the log in MultiView) and
@{b}Stop...@{ub} (stops the daemon, after asking - the window's close
gadget just closes the window). The window also carries menus
on the right mouse button: @{b}Project@{ub} (Open Log Amiga+L,
About..., Close Window, Stop AmiSync... Amiga+Q) and @{b}Sync@{ub}
(Rescan All Amiga+R, Pause All Amiga+P, Resume All Amiga+U). The lists scroll
when they overflow; resize the window freely with the size gadget -
once you do, it keeps your size.

Its commands also appear in Workbench's @{b}Tools menu@{ub} (an "AmiSync"
sub-menu on OS 3.9/3.2, flat entries on older systems):

  o  @{b}Status...@{ub}    open the status window
  o  @{b}Rescan All@{ub}   re-scan the folders now
  o  @{b}Pause All@{ub}    stop dialling peers
  o  @{b}Resume All@{ub}   resume dialling peers
  o  @{b}Stop...@{ub}      stop the daemon (asks first)

The one-line status is also exported to @{b}ENV:amisync/status@{ub}.

When AmiSync starts from User-Startup the icon and menu appear a couple
of seconds after the desktop loads. Set @{b}appicon = no@{ub} in the
config to turn all of this off. To re-skin the icon, replace
ENVARC:Amisync/appicon.info.

@endnode

@node ARexx "ARexx control"
@toc Main
AmiSync publishes an ARexx port named @{b}AMISYNC@{ub}. From an ARexx
script (set OPTIONS RESULTS to read a returned string):

@{b}STATUS@{ub}
   Returns a sectioned report: this device (uptime, traffic totals),
   folders (files, size, last scan) and remote devices (state, identity,
   traffic).

@{b}DISCOVERED@{ub}
   Lists unconfigured devices seen on the LAN. AmiSync never connects to
   them on its own.

@{b}ADDPEER <id> [host[:port]]@{ub}
   Add a device as a sync peer at runtime and append a peer line to
   S:amisync.conf. Given a host it is dialled right away; given the ID
   alone it waits to be found by local discovery.

@{b}ADDFOLDER <id> <path> [sendreceive|sendonly|receiveonly]@{ub}
   Add a folder at runtime and append its line to S:amisync.conf. The
   drawer is created if needed, scanned, then offered to every
   configured peer. (The status window's Add... button is the same
   thing with a requester; use ADDFOLDER for a custom id.)

@{b}REMOVEFOLDER <id>@{ub}
   Stop syncing folder <id> and remove its config line. Local files
   stay on disk and peers keep their copies.

@{b}RESCAN@{ub}
   Re-scan all folders now (normally automatic: within seconds of a
   change, plus a periodic scan).

@{b}PAUSE [id]@{ub} / @{b}RESUME [id]@{ub}
   Stop or resume dialling all peers, or just one. The id must be the
   peer's FULL device ID - the 7-character prefix STATUS shows will not
   match, and the command still reports success.

@{b}LOGLEVEL [debug|info|warn|error]@{ub}
   Set the log verbosity, or return the current level with no argument.

@{b}VERSION@{ub}   Return the version string.
@{b}HELP@{ub}      Return the command list.
@{b}QUIT@{ub}      Stop the daemon cleanly.

Example:

   options results
   address AMISYNC 'STATUS'
   say result

@endnode

@node Config "Configuration"
@toc Main
Settings live in @{b}S:amisync.conf@{ub}, one "key = value" per line.
A ';' or '#' begins a comment line (there are no inline comments).
See the installed amisync.conf.example for a fully annotated template.

@{b}General@{ub}
   logfile   = T:amisync.log     where the log is written
   logmax    = 256               log size cap in KB (0 = no cap)
   loglevel  = info              debug | info | warn | error
   seriallog = no                tee the log to the serial port
   appicon   = yes               show the Workbench AppIcon and menu
   rexxport  = AMISYNC           ARexx port name

@{b}Identity@{ub}
   certfile   = ENVARC:Amisync/cert.pem
   keyfile    = ENVARC:Amisync/key.pem
   devicename = amisync           the name peers see (sample: amiga4000)
   statedir   = ENVARC:Amisync/state
   keepdeletes = 180             days to keep deletion records

@{b}Networking@{ub}
   listenport = 22000            inbound BEP port (0 = dial only)
   discovery  = yes              local discovery on UDP 21027

@{b}Safety@{ub}
   versioning = no               keep replaced/deleted files
                                 (see @{" Versioning " link Version})

@{b}Clock@{ub}
   tzoffset   = +02:00           how far AHEAD of UTC this clock is
                                 set (east positive). An Amiga clock
                                 holds local time and the protocol
                                 speaks UTC, so without this the file
                                 dates you exchange are an offset out.
                                 Taken from Locale prefs by default;
                                 set it here only if Locale is unset
                                 or does not match this clock. '+00:00'
                                 means the clock really keeps UTC.
                                 No daylight-saving rule (Locale has
                                 none either), so a fixed offset is an
                                 hour out for half the year. Changing
                                 it is safe once syncing: files a peer
                                 sent are re-stamped, yours are
                                 announced once with the right time.

@{b}Peers@{ub} (one line each; up to 16)
   peer = <device-id> [<host>[:<port>]]

@{b}Folders@{ub} (up to 8)
   folder = <id> <path> [sendreceive|sendonly|receiveonly] [label]

Double-quote a path or label that contains spaces, e.g.
@{b}folder = photos "Ram Disk:Photos" sendreceive@{ub}.

The folder id must match the id in Syncthing; the path is the local
AmigaOS directory. A .stignore file in the folder root is honoured
(Syncthing-style patterns).

@{b}receiveonly is a mirror.@{ub} Files you create in such a folder stay
there - AmiSync never announces them and never removes them. Files that
arrived from the peer belong to the peer: if you edit one, the peer's
copy replaces your edit at the next scan, because this mode has no way
to send a change back and so no way to reconcile one.

Your edit is not lost. The copy being replaced is moved to the folder's
.stversions drawer first - whether or not versioning is switched on -
and the log names the file. Copy it back out if you wanted it. Use
sendreceive for a folder you intend to edit in.

@endnode

@node Version "File versioning"
@toc Main
By default, when a remote change deletes or overwrites a file, the old
copy on the Amiga is gone. Turn on @{b}versioning@{ub} to keep a safety
net:

   versioning = yes

With it on, a file a remote change is about to overwrite or delete is
first moved into a @{b}.stversions@{ub} drawer at the folder root,
keeping only its most recent old copy (Syncthing's "trash can" mode).

This protects you from a mistaken delete or edit made on another device.
To restore a file, copy it back out of .stversions. The drawer is never
synced to peers.

Concurrent edits (both sides change the same file at once) are handled
separately: the losing copy is kept as a *.sync-conflict-* file whether
or not versioning is on.

@endnode

@node Trouble "Troubleshooting"
@toc Main
@{b}It says "Offline" and never connects.@{ub}
Check that AmiSSL and a TCP/IP stack are installed and running, that the
peer's device ID and address in S:amisync.conf are correct, and that the
peer's Syncthing has accepted this device and shared the folder. The log
(T:amisync.log by default) usually says why.

@{b}"peer is X, expected Y".@{ub}
The certificate at that address did not match the configured device ID.
Confirm you have the right ID and that nothing else is answering on that
host and port.

@{b}"cannot bind UDP 21027".@{ub}
Something else holds the discovery port (e.g. another Syncthing on the
same machine, under emulation). Discovery send still works; only receive
is affected.

@{b}A file will not sync.@{ub}
Very long names can exceed what FFS stores (30 characters per component);
use PFS3 or SFS for such folders. A folder also nests at most 12 levels
deep - entries deeper than that are skipped. Check the log too for a
"too large" or "unsafe name" note.

@{b}More detail.@{ub}
Set @{b}loglevel = debug@{ub} (or send the ARexx LOGLEVEL debug command)
and watch T:amisync.log.

@endnode

